.TH codata_lib 3 "@DATE@" "@VERSION@" "Library calls"

.SH NAME
\"{{{
codata \- libray for fundamental physical constants
\"}}}

.SH LIBRARY
\"{{{
Codata library 
.RI ( libcodata ", " \-lcodata )
\"}}}

.SH SYNOPSIS
\"{{{
.IP Fortran 10 
.B use codata\fP

.IP C 10
.B #include \(dqcodata.h\(dq

.IP Python 10
.B import pycodata
\"}}}

.SH DESCRIPTION
\"{{{
.PP
\fBcodata\fP is a Fortran library providing the fundamental physical
constants published by the CODATA.
It includes the 2022, 2018, 2014 and 2010 recommended values, 
allowing applications to reproduce calculations based on specific 
CODATA releases.
.PP
The constants are implemented as a derived type that carries:
.I the name, the value, the uncertainty, and the unit.
All the constants are provided as \fBdouble precision reals\fP.
Long constant names can be aliased with shorter names.
.PP
It provides:
.IP \(bu 5 
CODATA 2022 constants
.IP \(bu 5 
CODATA 2018 constants
.IP \(bu 5 
CODATA 2014 constants
.IP \(bu 5 
CODATA 2010 constants
.IP \(bu 5 
Native Fortran API
.IP \(bu 5 
C interoperability API
.IP \(bu 5 
Python bindings
.IP \(bu 5 
Selection of an adjustment at compile time or at run time

.TP
.B
Fortran API
exposes the constants as derived type \fBcodata_constant_type\fP
which carries 
.I the name, the value, the uncertainty, and the unit.
.TP
.B
C API
exposes a structure \fBcodata_constant_type\fP that defines
the same members as in Fortran i.e.
.I the name, the value, the uncertainty, and the unit.

.TP
.B
Python
exposes a dictionary with the keys being 
.I the name, the value, the uncertainty, and the unit.
\"}}}

.SH SELECTING AN ADJUSTMENT
\"{{{
.PP
There are two ways to reach a constant, and they are generated from the
same source data, so they cannot disagree.
.TP
.B
Named symbols, selected at compile time
Every adjustment is available under year-suffixed names:
.BR BOHR_RADIUS_2010 ,
.BR BOHR_RADIUS_2014 ,
.BR BOHR_RADIUS_2018 ,
.BR BOHR_RADIUS_2022 .
These are
.B parameters
in Fortran and
.B const
objects in C, so they may be used in initialisation expressions and array
bounds, and are folded into the code by the compiler. Use these when the
adjustment is fixed when the program is built.
.TP
.B
The runtime accessor
.B codata_value(ds, q)
and its companions take the adjustment
.I ds
and the quantity key
.I q
as arguments and resolve the constant at run time. Use these when the
adjustment is chosen by input, or when a program must hold more than one
adjustment at once, such as reading a checkpoint written under an older
adjustment while computing under a current one.
.PP
The adjustment is always an explicit argument. There is no global
\(dqcurrent adjustment\(dq to set, so the accessors are reentrant and two
adjustments can be in use at the same time.
.PP
The available adjustments are
.BR CODATA_2010 ,
.BR CODATA_2014 ,
.BR CODATA_2018 ,
.BR CODATA_2022 ,
and
.BR CODATA_LATEST ,
which follows the most recent one and therefore changes when a new
adjustment is added.
\"}}}

.SH QUANTITY KEYS
\"{{{
.PP
A quantity key names a physical quantity independently of the adjustment
it is taken from. The keys are
.B CODATA_Q_*
in C and Python and
.B Q_*
in Fortran; the Fortran prefix is shorter because Fortran limits a name to
63 characters, which
.B CODATA_Q_
plus the longest quantity name exceeds.
.PP
Key values never change when a new adjustment is added, so a key stored in
a file remains valid.
.PP
The adjustments do not all contain the same quantities. Use
.B codata_defined(ds, q)
to test, or
.B codata_get(ds, q)
which returns
.B NULL
(a null pointer in Fortran, None in Python) for a quantity that adjustment
does not contain.
.B codata_value
returns a quiet
.B NaN
in that case, never 0.0: a plausible-looking wrong number is the worst
available failure mode for a constants library.
\"}}}

.SH RENAMED QUANTITIES
\"{{{
.PP
NIST renames quantities between adjustments. What CODATA 2014 calls
.B ELECTRIC_CONSTANT
is called
.B VACUUM_ELECTRIC_PERMITTIVITY
from 2018 on, and
.B PLANCK_CONSTANT_OVER_2_PI
became
.BR REDUCED_PLANCK_CONSTANT .
.PP
Every such quantity is available under both spellings in every
adjustment, and both spellings share one quantity key, so code written
against either name works against all adjustments.
.PP
A pair is only treated as a rename when the units are compatible and the
values agree to within five standard uncertainties. Names alone are not
enough:
.B ELEMENTARY_CHARGE_OVER_H
and
.B ELEMENTARY_CHARGE_OVER_H_BAR
look like a rename but differ by a factor of 2\(*p. Quantities that were
genuinely retired or genuinely added are not aliased, and are reported as
undefined for the adjustments that lack them.
\"}}}

.SH NOTES
\"{{{
.PP
The latest adjustment is additionally available without the year as a
suffix, as an alias meaning \(dqcurrent\(dq. Because that alias follows
the most recent adjustment, it changes value when a new adjustment is
added; use the year-suffixed names to pin to a particular adjustment.
All constants are declared with the same variable name in Fortran, C,
and Python.

